Supreme People's Court

Provisions on Providing Judicial Assistance to Parties with Genuine Economic Difficulties

(Adopted at the 1124th Session of the Judicial Committee of the Supreme People's Court on July 12, 2000, and revised at the 1347th Session of the Judicial Committee of the Supreme People's Court on April 5, 2005)

Fa Fa [2005] No. 6

Article 1  These Provisions are formulated in accordance with the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, and the Measures for the Collection of Litigation Costs by the People's Courts, for the purpose of enabling parties with genuine economic difficulties to exercise their litigation rights according to law and to protect their lawful rights and interests.

Article 2  For the purpose of these Provisions, "judicial assistance" refers to the deferment, reduction, or exemption of litigation costs granted by the people's court to a party who, for the purpose of safeguarding his or her own lawful rights and interests, brings a civil or administrative lawsuit before the people's court but has genuine economic difficulties.

Article 3  A party who satisfies the requirements of Article 2 of these Provisions and falls under any of the following circumstances may apply to the people's court for judicial assistance:

(1) claiming maintenance for parents, support for children, alimony, or pension for the disabled or for the family of the deceased;
(2) elderly persons living alone, orphans, and rural households enjoying the "five guarantees" (food, clothing, medical care, housing, and burial expenses);
(3) disabled persons or persons suffering from serious illnesses who have no regular source of income;
(4) recipients of special care and preferential treatment or persons entitled to resettlement as prescribed by the State;
(5) claiming social insurance benefits, labor remuneration, or economic compensation;
(6) victims of traffic accidents, medical accidents, work-related accidents, product quality accidents, or other personal injury accidents who claim compensation;
(7) persons whose lawful rights and interests have been damaged as a result of performing just and courageous acts or protecting the public interest, and who, together with their close relatives, claim compensation or economic remedies;
(8) migrant workers claiming labor remuneration or claiming compensation for other infringements of their lawful rights and interests;
(9) persons who are currently receiving the minimum subsistence allowance for urban residents, relief for rural households in extreme poverty, or unemployment insurance benefits, and who have no other income;
(10) persons who, due to natural disasters or other force majeure, are living in difficulty, are receiving social relief, or whose family production or business operations are unsustainable;
(11) persons who bring a lawsuit against an administrative organ for unlawfully requiring farmers to perform obligations;
(12) persons who are currently receiving legal aid from the relevant authorities;
(13) parties that are social welfare institutions, homes for the elderly, special care hospitals, psychiatric hospitals, SOS Children's Villages, social relief stations, special education institutions, or other social public welfare units;
(14) other circumstances where judicial assistance is genuinely needed.

Article 4  A party requesting judicial assistance from the people's court shall, at the time of filing the complaint or appeal, submit a written application and sufficient evidentiary materials to prove the genuine economic difficulty. Where the application for judicial assistance is based on living difficulties or claiming basic living expenses, the party shall provide a certificate that the economic conditions of the party and his or her family meet the standards for economic difficulty prescribed by the local civil affairs, labor, and social security authorities.

Article 5  Upon examination, if the people's court finds that a party's request for judicial assistance falls under any of the circumstances listed in Article 3 of these Provisions, the court shall, at the time of case filing, permit the party to defer the payment of litigation costs.

Article 6  Where the people's court decides to grant judicial assistance to one party, and the opposing party loses the case, the litigation costs shall be paid by the opposing party; if the opposing party refuses to pay, enforcement shall be carried out compulsorily.

Where the opposing party wins the case, the court may decide to reduce or exempt the litigation costs for the party who applied for judicial assistance, based on the economic conditions of that party. Where a decision is made to reduce the litigation costs, the reduction ratio shall not be less than 30%. Where the circumstances fall under Items (2) or (9) of Article 3 of these Provisions, the litigation costs shall be exempted.

Article 7  Where a party requests deferment of litigation costs, the judge or collegial panel handling the case shall submit an opinion and report it to the division chief for approval; where a party requests reduction or exemption of litigation costs, the judge or collegial panel handling the case shall submit an opinion, and after review and consent by the division chief, report it to the president of the court for approval.

Article 8  Where the people's court decides to reduce or exempt litigation costs for a party, the decision shall be set forth in the legal document.

Article 9  Where a party obtains judicial assistance by fraud, the people's court shall order the party to make up the litigation costs; if the party refuses to do so, the matter shall be treated as an act obstructing litigation.

Article 10  These Provisions shall come into force as of the date of promulgation.

April 5, 2005
